  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 337 AM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nWe remain in a quasi-zonal pattern this morning, with seasonably \nfast flow over the norther tier states and upper ridging to our \nsouth. At the surface, a stationary front has set up across Nebraska \nand Iowa, on the southern periphery of the stronger jet. The warmest \ntemperature anomalies across the country are focused just south of \nthis boundary, where hot southwesterly 850 mb flow is present. As is \ntypical, the hottest temperatures and lowest dewpoints will remain \ntowards central KS where mixing is greatest. Farther east, \ntemperatures will stay slightly cooler but dewpoints stay in the \n70s. As such, heat indices should climb into the 100s across the \nentire area. Have extended the Heat Advisory slightly farther north \nas a result. Similar to yesterday, persistent high clouds and an \noccasional sprinkle or two may linger across northern KS and keep \ntemperatures down a few degrees, but it will be hot regardless.\n\nTomorrow will be more of the same. Temperatures approach 100 across \nmost of the area, with heat indices above 100 again. Will likely \nneed another Heat Advisory for parts of the area, but will wait a \nbit to see trends in cloud cover across northern KS. As the \nstationary front to our north begins to inch south, we'll likely see \nanother round of scattered showers and weak storms along the \nboundary. Low chance this occurs as early as the daytime Wednesday, \nbut more likely overnight Wednesday as isentropic ascent increases \nnear the Nebraska border. Storms should remain high-based and \nscattered, keeping the overall severe threat low, but can't rule out \nan occasional strong wind gust or small hail.\n\nThursday into Saturday, the front will only slowly inch southward \nacross the area. It should remain quite hot south of the boundary, \nwith heat indices still above 100. North of the boundary \ntemperatures will be more dependent on cloud cover but still should \nclimb into the low 90s each day. Upper support for convection will \nbe more limited, but scattered showers and storms will remain \npossible each day towards northern KS. Guidance has come to a better \nconsensus that the front will finally get a stronger southward push \nby Sunday. There are still differences in exactly how much cooler \nthe airmass behind the front will be, but we should see temperatures \nget back closer to average for the first part of next week.",
